.TH LIBSOCKFIX 1M "28 November 2026" "libsockfix 1.0-1"
.SH NAME
libsockfix \- the socket libraries without their per-socket heap leak
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /opt/amix/sbin/libsockfix status
.br
.B /opt/amix/sbin/libsockfix link
.br
.B /opt/amix/sbin/libsockfix unfix
.SH DESCRIPTION
The libsockfix package replaces the system's two socket libraries,
.B /usr/lib/libsockhost.so
(names from
.BR /etc/hosts )
and
.B /usr/lib/libsockdns.so
(names also from DNS), with the stock files changed in one place.
.B /usr/lib/libsocket.so ,
the name programs load, is a link to one of the two.
.PP
The stock library keeps a buffer for each socket it creates.
When a program closes a socket with
.BR close (2),
which is the C library's and not the socket library's, the buffer
stays behind; the next
.BR socket (3N),
.BR accept (3N)
or DNS lookup that gets the same descriptor number allocates a new
buffer and loses the old one.
Each lost buffer is about 200 bytes for an Internet socket and 4.4
Kbytes for a UNIX-domain socket, and the program's data grows by that
much until it exits.
A server that runs for a long time grows without limit: an X server
kept by
.BR dtlogin (1X)
by about 1 Mbyte for a day of desktop use, inetd and dropbear by about
200 bytes a connection.
With the fixed library the old buffer is freed before the new one is
allocated.
Everything else is the stock library, byte for byte.
.PP
The
.B libsockfix
command shows the state of the libraries and does the work of the
package's installation scripts; an administrator needs only
.BR status .
.TP
.B status
Shows whether
.B libsocket.so
is the DNS library or the hosts-only one, and whether each of the two
files holds the stock or the fixed bytes.
.TP
.B link
Run by the package's postinstall.
Links
.B libsocket.so
to the fixed file of the variant it is: to
.B libsockdns.so
when it was that file (DNS on), or has its bytes, and to
.B libsockhost.so
otherwise.
The link is made under a temporary name and renamed over
.BR libsocket.so ,
so the name is never missing while programs start.
The contents database records the result.
.TP
.B unfix
Run by the package's preremove.
Puts the stock bytes back into both files, each by rename, and links
.B libsocket.so
to the stock file of the variant it is at that moment, then records the
three files.
After
.BR pkgrm (1M)
the system has the files exactly as the core package installed them.
.PP
Turning DNS on or off is amix-base's
.BR ifup (1M)
as before: it links
.B libsocket.so
to
.B libsockdns.so
at every boot when a name server is set, and with the package installed
that file is the fixed one.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/lib/libsockhost.so, /usr/lib/libsockdns.so
the fixed libraries
.TP
.B /opt/amix/lib/libsockfix/host.stock, dns.stock
the 60 stock bytes of each library's changed range, which
.B unfix
writes back
.TP
.B /opt/amix/doc/libsockfix/README.md
the changed bytes, the reasons and the tests
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B unfix
leaves a library that is neither the stock nor the fixed file as it is,
says so, and exits 1.
.B link
and
.B unfix
need apkg's
.BR installf (1M)
and
.BR removef (1M)
in
.BR /usr/apkg/bin ;
the stock
.B installf
faults.
.SH NOTES
Programs that are already running keep the library they started with
until they exit.
.PP
Programs linked with the archive
.B /usr/lib/libsocket.a
keep the stock code.
On a standard system only the DHCP client and apkg are, and neither
creates sockets in a loop.
.PP
.BR pkgchk (1M)
compares
.B /usr/lib/libsocket.so
with the bytes recorded when the package last linked it.
When
.BR ifup (1M)
later turns DNS on or off, it reports the file as changed, as it does
on the stock system with DNS on.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifup (1M),
.BR pkgrm (1M),
.BR socket (3N)
